{#
  Year-by-year placement volume, from _data/growth.json.

  Why a chart and not a headline counter: a dated series can be checked, and a round
  "2,400+ placed" can't. This is the one proof structure worth taking from the
  competitor research wholesale (see PRD.md) — it out-performs a bigger number
  precisely because it's smaller and specific.

  Columns rather than the horizontal bars this section used to carry. The data's job
  here is change-over-time, and a rising left-to-right silhouette states that in one
  glance; stacked horizontal bars made the same numbers read as a settings list. The
  headline stat to the left gives the series a single answer for the reader who won't
  study six figures, and the chart is the evidence behind it.

  The markup is still a <dl>: year is a term, figure is its definition, and both are
  real text. With CSS off this degrades to a readable year/number list, which is the
  whole point of a section whose argument is auditability. That also means the chart
  needs no separate table view — the values are already text, and every one of them is
  labelled rather than hidden behind a tooltip.
#}
{% set maxPlaced = 0 %}
{% for row in growth.list %}
  {% if row.placed > maxPlaced %}{% set maxPlaced = row.placed %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{# The last complete year gets the solid fill; the year before it gives us the change.
   Any row carrying a note (e.g. "Year to date") is a part-year and must never be
   highlighted as a total or used as the basis of a comparison. #}
{% set currentYear = null %}
{% set currentPlaced = 0 %}
{% set prevYear = null %}
{% set prevPlaced = 0 %}
{% for row in growth.list %}
  {% if not row.note %}
    {% set prevYear = currentYear %}
    {% set prevPlaced = currentPlaced %}
    {% set currentYear = row.year %}
    {% set currentPlaced = row.placed %}
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}

<div class="flex flex-col gap-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-end sm:justify-between">
  <div class="section-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">By the year</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Placements, one year at a time.</h2>
  </div>
  <p class="meta max-w-xs leading-relaxed">
    {{ growth.unitLabel }} · figures under review
  </p>
</div>

<div class="glass-panel mt-8 p-6 sm:p-8">
  <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,14rem)_1fr] lg:items-end lg:gap-12">

    {# The single answer, for the reader who won't read six numbers. Deliberately not
       the part-year figure — this is the last year we can stand behind as a total. #}
    <div class="lg:pb-10">
      <p class="meta">Latest full year</p>
      <p class="growth-headline">{{ currentPlaced }}</p>
      {% if prevPlaced %}
      <p class="growth-delta">
        <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span>
        {{ ((currentPlaced - prevPlaced) / prevPlaced * 100) | round }}%
        <span class="sr-only">increase</span>
        vs {{ prevYear }}
      </p>
      {% endif %}
      <p class="body-copy mt-4 text-sm">
        Every figure below is a counted placement, not an application or a shortlist.
      </p>
    </div>

    <dl class="growth-chart">
      {% for row in growth.list %}
      <div class="growth-col"
           {% if row.year == currentYear %}data-current{% endif %}
           {% if row.note %}data-partial{% endif %}>
        <dt class="growth-year">
          {{ row.year }}
          {# The part-year marker is text, not a lighter fill. A reader who can't
             separate the hatched column from the solid ones still gets the caveat. #}
          {% if row.note %}
          <span class="growth-tag" title="{{ row.note }}">YTD</span>
          <span class="sr-only">— {{ row.note }}</span>
          {% endif %}
        </dt>
        <dd class="growth-plot">
          <span class="growth-value">{{ row.placed }}</span>
          <span class="growth-bar" style="height: {{ (row.placed / maxPlaced * 100) | round }}%"></span>
        </dd>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </dl>

  </div>
</div>
